    "ls|LS16_S8_U_187": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 187\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 06TH MAY, 2016\nMENTAL HEALTH INSTITUTES\n*187. SHRIMATI RITA TARAI:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to\nstate:\n(a) whether cases of mental disorder are on the rise in the country in the recent past,\nif so, the details thereof;\n(b) whether any study/survey has been undertaken to find out the causes of sudden\nrise in mental and neurological disorders, if so, the details thereof;\n(c) whether the Government has any plan/proposes to formulate any plan for the\nupgradation of Mental Health Institutes into super specialist institute of mental\nhealth and neurosciences at par with the National Institute of Mental Health and\nNeurosciences (NIMHANS);\n(d) if so, the details thereof, State/UT wise including Odisha; and\n(e) the follow-up action taken by the Government in this regard?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI JAGAT PRAKASH NADDA)\n(a) to (e):  A statement is laid on the Table of the House\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 187* FOR 06TH MAY, 2016\nIn 2005, the National Commission on Macroeconomics and Health reported\nthat 10-20 million (1-2% of population) suffered from severe mental disorders such\nas schizophrenia and bipolar disorder and nearly 50 million (5% of population) from\ncommon mental disorders such as depression and anxiet y, yielding an overall\nestimate of 6.5 per cent of the population.\nThe Government has commissioned a National Mental Health Survey through\nthe National Institute of Mental Health and Neuro Sciences (NIMHANS), Bangalore\nwith, inter-alia, the following objectives:\n• estimate the prevalence and burden of Mental, Neurological and substance\nuse  disorders in a representative population of India,\n• identify the health care seeking and services utilization patterns, and\n• assess mental health care resources and facilit ies in the surveyed states for\nplanning, and strengthening of resources and services.\nThe survey was initiated on 1st June, 2015 and about 27000 interviews were\ncompleted at the field level as on 5th April, 2016.\nDuring the 12th Five Year Plan period, Government has approved a scheme for\nupgradation of two Central Government mental health institutes namely Lokopriya\nGopinath Bordoloi Regional Institute of Mental Health (LGBRIMH), Tezpur,\nAssam and Central Institute of Psychiatry, Ranchi to provide neurological and\nneuro-surgical facilities on the pattern of National Institute of Mental Health and\nNeurosciences (NIMHANS), Bangalore. The Government does not have any scheme\nfor upgradation of State Government mental health institutes into super specialist\ninstitute of mental health and neurosciences at par with NIMHANS, Bangalore.\nHowever, Government has provided financial assistance for establishment of\nCentres of Excellence in the field of mental health at 15 Institutes belonging to the\nState Governments/UT Administrations including Odisha. Information relating to\nthe funds released for establishment of Centres of Excellence is annexed.\n\nANNEXURE\nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 187 FOR\nANSWER ON 06.05.2016 REGARDING “MENTAL HEALTH INSTITUTES”\nDetails of funds released for establishment of Centres of Excellence in the field of mental\nhealth\nS.No. Mental Hospital/ Institute Amount Released\n1. Institute of Mental Health & Hospital, Agra, Uttar Pradesh Rs. 28,81,00,000/-\n2. Hospital for Mental Health, Ahmedabad, Gujarat Rs. 24,79,00,000/-\n3. State Mental H ealth Institute, Pandit Bhagwat Dayal Sharma\nUniversity of Health Sciences, Rohtak, Haryana\nRs. 26,36,38,788/-\n4. Institute of Psychiatry- Kolkata, West Bengal Rs. 24,90,00,000/-\n5. Institute of Mental Health, Hyderabad, Andhra Pradesh Rs. 5,28,00,000/-\n6. Psychiatric Diseases Hospital, Government Medical College,\nSrinagar, Jammu & Kashmir\nRs. 28,84,00,000/-\n7. Department of Psychiatry, Govt. Medical College, Chandigarh Rs. 18,59,00,000/-\n8. Mental Health Institute, SCB Medical College, Cuttack Rs. 27,78,00,000/-\n9 Institute of Mental Health and Neuro -Sciences, Kozhikode,\nKerala\nRs. 28,84,00,000/-\n10. Institute of Human Behaviour and Allied Sciences, Shahdra,\nDelhi\nRs. 5,28,00,000/-\n11. Maharashtra Institute of Mental Health, Pune Rs. 30,00,00,000/-\n12. Dharwad Institute of Mental Health and Neuro Sciences,\nDharwad, Karnataka\nRs. 5,00,00,000/-\n13. Institute of Psychiatry and Human Behaviour, Bambolim, Goa Rs. 4,76,75,000/-\n14. Gwalior Mansik Arogyashala, Gwalior, Madhya Pradesh Rs. 4,75,75,,000/-\n15. Institute of Mental Health, Amritsar, Punjab Rs. 4,75,76,000/-\nTotal Rs. 256,24,64,788/-",
    "ls|LS16_S8_U_188": "adolescent minds and promote immoral cultural ethos . The Press should refuse to accept such \nadvertisements. \n \nxiv) Classified advertisements of health and physic al fitness services using undignified languages, \nindicative of covert soliciting, are violative of l aw as well as ethics. The newspaper should adopt a \nmechanism for vetting such an advertisement to ensu re that the soliciting advertisements are not \ncarried. \n \nxv) Advertisements of contraceptive and supply of b rand item attaching to the advertisement is not \nvery ethical, given the social milieu and the tradi tional values held dear in our country. A newspaper  \nhas a sacred duty to educate people about precautio nary measures to avoid AIDS and exhibit greater \nfar sight in accepting advertisement even though issued by social welfare organisation. \n \n(xvi) Employment News which is trusted as a purveyor of authentic news on government jobs should \nbe more careful in accepting advertisements of only bonafide private bodies. \n \n(xvii) While accepting advertisements of educationa l institutes newspapers may ensure that such \nadvertisements carry the mandatory statement that t he concerned institutes are recognized under the \nrelevant enactments of law.  \n \nxviii) Advertisements play extremely vital role in shaping the values and concerns of the present day \nsociety and as more and more lenient view is taken of what is not the norm, the speedier may be \nacceptability of such matters in ‘public perception ’ but at what cost is the essential point for \nconsideration. It should be borne in mind that in t he race to be globally relevant we do not leave \nbehind the values that have earned India the unique  place it enjoys globally on moral and ethical \nplane. \n \n \n*****  \n\nANNEXURE-IV  \n \nANNEXURE AS REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (e ) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 188 FOR 06.05.2016  \n \n \nComplaint cases relating to allegedly Misleading Advertisements (Excluding cases disposed for \nnot being perused by the Complainant)  \n \n2013-2014  \n \nS.No. Complainant Respondent Subject Action Taken/S tatus \n1. Sh. Deepak Chhabria , \nChairman, Employment \nPromotion Council of \nIndian Personnel, 105, Sai \nPrasad Commercial \nComplex, Khar, Mumbai \n– 400052 \nEmployment \n& NRI Times \nPublication of \nunauthorized overseas \nrecruitment \npublication \nClosed on 12 .0 7.13 \nwith reiteration of the \nprevious adjudication \n/ guidelines of the \nrespondent. \n2. Shri Arvind Kumar, \nUnder Secretary, M/o \nOverseas Indian Affairs \nEmigration Section, \nAkbar Bhavan, \nChanakyapuri, New Delhi \n \nRashtra \nDeepika \nIssuing \nadvertisements for \noverseas employment \nby unregistered \nrecruiting agent in \nviolation of the \nEmigration Act, 1983 \nClosed with \nreiteration of the \nprevious adjudication \n/ guidelines of the \nrespondent.  \n \n2014-2015  \n \nS. No  Complaint Respondent Subject Action Taken/Status \n \n----NIL----- \n \n \n \n2015-2016 \n \n \nS. No  Complaint Respondent Subject Action Taken/Status \n \n----NIL----- \n \n \n******** ",
    "ls|LS16_S8_U_189": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 189\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 25.04.2016\n \n \nJOB PORTAL FOR MSME\n \n \n189.\n \n \nSHRI PRALHAD JOSHI:\n \n \n \nWill the Minister of MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES be pleased to state:\n \n \n \n(\na\n)\n \nthe details of number of persons registered as job seeker, and as employers \nwith the job portal, ‘Employment Exchange for Industries’, launched by the \nGovernment; \n \n(\nb\n)\n \nwhether the \ndifferent jobs available have been listed on the website;\n \n(\nc\n)\n \nif so, the details thereof and the number of persons actually applied for a job \nthrough the portal, if not, the reasons therefor;\n \n(\nd\n)\n \nwhether the portal lists jobs in the manufacturing as well as the se\nrvices sector, \nif not, the reasons therefor; and \n \n(\ne\n)\n \nwhether the Government plans to tie up with other private job portals and if \nso, the details thereof?\n \n \n \nANSWER \n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF \n \nMICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n(SHRI GIRIRAJ \nSINGH)\n \n \n(a) :\n \n    \n3,45,024 job seekers and 2933 employers have registered on the portal \nnamely “Employment Exchange for Industries” as on 18\nth\n \nApril 2016 \n \n \n(b)& (c):\n \n     \nPresently, the main objective of the portal is to facilitate matchmaking \nbetween job \nseekers and employers for their mutual benefits. Very few employers have \nnotified their vacancies on the portal at present.\n \n \n(d):\n \n       \nMainly the employers belonging to manufacturing sector are using the \ninformation available on the portal.  However, the\n \nemployers from service sector can \nalso use the portal.\n \n \n(e): \n \n      \nWith the launch of National Career Service (NCS) by Ministry of Labour \n& Employment, there is a need for convergence of job portal for MSME (Employment \nExchange for Industries) with the N\national Career Service.\n \n \n \n********\n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S8_U_191": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 191\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 06TH MAY, 2016\nRESPIRATORY DISEASES\n†*191. SHRI SUKHBIR SINGH JAUNAPURIA:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to\nstate:\n(a) whether respiratory ailments are on the rise in the country, if so, the details\nthereof and the reasons therefor, State/ UT-wise;\n(b) the number of cases of respiratory ailments reported during the las t three years,\nState/UT-wise;\n(c) whether the Government has conducted any study/survey to assess the\nrespiratory health status of people, if so, the details thereof and the outcome thereon;\nand\n(d) the preventive measures taken by the Government in this regard?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI JAGAT PRAKASH NADDA)\n(a) to (d):  A statement is laid on the Table of the House\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 191* FOR 06TH MAY, 2016\n(a) to (d) There is a slight increase in the cases of the Acute Respiratory Infection\n(ARI) as reported by the Central Bureau of Health Intelligence (CBHI). The details\nof the cases reported due to ARI during 2013, 2014 and 2015 are placed at\nAnnexure.\nNo Nationwide survey has been conducted to assess the respiratory health status of\nthe people. As per a survey conducted by Vallabbhai Patel Chest Institute, Delhi in\n2006, which covered 5900 adults belonging to urban, rural and slum population in\nDelhi , 11.69% were found to be suffering from Rhinits and 11.03% from Asthma.\nThe measures being taken to prevent respiratory ailments include the following:\n1. Government has taken various measures to control environ mental pollution\nincluding tightening of vehicular and industrial norms, promotion of cleaner\ntechnologies, strengthening of network of air quality monitoring stations,\npromoting public awareness etc.\n2. Under the Integrated Disease Surveillance Programme, th e Districts and\nStates have been strengthened by providing manpower, training of identified\nRapid Response Team (RRT) members for outbreak investigations,\nstrengthening of laboratories for detection of epidemic prone diseases\nincluding acute respiratory infections.\n3. Under Reproductive and Child Health Programme, prevention and treatment\nof acute respiratory infection including pneumonia is being addressed on\npriority basis.\n4. For prevention and control of Tuberculosis, Revised National Tuberculosis\nControl Pro gramme (RNTCP) is being implemented under National Health\nMission (NHM).\n\nAnnexure\nState/UT-wise Cases due to Acute Respiratory Infection (ARI) reported during the years 2013–2015\nS.No. State /U.T 2013 2014\n(Provisional)\n2015\n(Provisional)\nCases Cases Cases\n1 Andhra Pradesh 3110536 2413509 2096497\n2 Arunachal Pradesh 46643 17887 10910\n3 Assam 145047 68334 20456\n4 Bihar 1758655 1488744 1184411\n5 Chhattisgarh 287771 363570 375805\n6 Goa 59590 67720 60998\n7 Gujarat 1041042 1249351 1369389\n8 Haryana 1003454 1123370 1164780\n9 Himachal Pradesh 1631779 1523572 1591505\n10 Jammu & Kashmir 789714 746222 762025\n11 Jharkhand 238206 224576 201591\n12 Karnataka 1577354 2213159 2118979\n13 Kerala 6233391 6042825 5879901\n14 Madhya Pradesh 1276515 1529711 1702426\n15 Maharashtra 896949 1146185 1537325\n16 Manipur 31468 33470 37477\n17 Meghalaya 329019 375220 169854\n18 Mizoram 33807 28790 33169\n19 Nagaland 37664 38312 22529\n20 Odisha 1599506 2125240 2127921\n21 Punjab 692618 638908 612122\n22 Rajasthan 2515972 3038583 2822582\n23 Sikkim 93726 83893 53220\n24 Tamil Nadu 1944280 1904592 1962629\n25 Telangana NA NA 1137894\n26 Tripura 158988 154227 145522\n27 Uttarakhand 185034 211385 253292\n28 Uttar Pradesh 1720714 1627509 1772916\n29 West Bengal 2514606 2831623 2991474\n30 A & N Islands 58307 61716 44465\n31 Chandigarh 100541 109081 127479\n32 D & N Haveli 96346 102487 61639\n33 Daman & Diu 48603 60571 56483\n34 Delhi 390170 369406 307597\n35 Lakshadweep 43944 49807 37297\n36 Puducherry 731148 772188 795892\nTotal 33423107 34835743 35650451\nSource: Monthly Health Condition Reports from Directorate of Health Services of States/UTs\nNote:\n1. NA  stands for Not Available\n2. 2014: Andhra Pradesh Excludes data of 10 districts of Telangana from July 2014 Onwards.",
    "ls|LS16_S8_U_192": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT  \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION AND LITERACY  \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 192 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 25TH APRIL, 2016 \n \n \nNumber of Fake Schools \n \n \n†192. SHRIMATI RANJANBEN BHATT:  \n \n Will the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:  \n \n (a) whether it has come to the notice of the Government that number of schools in \nvarious parts of the country are functioning on papers only and if so, the details thereof, \nState/UT-wise;  \n \n (b) whether the Government has taken steps against such schools and if so, the details \nthereof; and  \n \n (c) if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(SMT. SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n \n(a):  Education being a concurrent subject, majority of schools and the State Examination \nBoards come under the purview of respective State Government/UT Administration. No data \nis maintained at Central level with regard to fake schools. \n \n(b) & (c): Do not arise.  \n \n   \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S8_U_193": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 193 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 25.04.2016 \n \nTeaching of Sanskrit in Schools \n \n†193. SHRI A.T. NANA PATIL: \n \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has formulated any scheme to teach Sanskrit at primary and \nmiddle school levels in various parts of the country; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; \n \n(c) whether it is a fact that many national and international organizations have mentioned \nthat Sanskrit language helps mental and cultural development of children; \n \n(d) if so, whether the Government has taken any steps in this regard; and \n \n(e) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(SMT. SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n \n(a) & (b) As per Three Language Formula, Sanskrit is taught as a third language (Modern \nIndian Language) across the country from class VI onwards.  In some states viz. Uttar Pradesh, \nUttarakhand and Odisha, Sanskrit is taught in primary stage in classes II/ III/ IV. \n \n(c) to (e) National Policy on Education (NPE) – 1986 (as modified in 1992) states that \nconsidering the special importance of Sanskrit to the growth and development of Indian \nlanguages and its unique contribution to the cultural unity of the country, facilities for it s \nteaching at the school and university stages should be offered on a more liberal scale. National \nCurriculum Framework (NCF) – 2005 states that Sanskrit is rich in its inflectional grammatical \nstructure and aesthetic value, and can illuminate our lives as  many languages keep borrowing \nwords from it. It further states that Sanskrit may also be studied as a Modern Indian Language \n(MIL).  \n  \n****** \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S8_U_194": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF AYURVEDA, YOGA & NATUROPATHY, UNANI, SIDDHA\nAND HOMOEOPATHY (AYUSH)\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 194\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 06TH MAY, 2016\nAYUSH INDUSTRY CLUSTERS\n*194. SHRI R. GOPALAKRISHNAN:\nSHRI RAGHAV LAKHANPAL:\nWill the Minister of AYURVEDA, YOGA AND NATUROPATHY,\nUNANI, SIDDHA AND HOMOEOPATHY (AYUSH) be pleased to state:\n(a) the details of the AYUSH Industry Clusters (AIC) set up in various parts of the\ncountry along with the funds allocated for each of these clusters, State-wise;\n(b) whether the Government has formulated any plan for setting up of more such\nclusters across the country, if so, the details thereof, State-wise;\n(c) whether any requests from States for setting up of such clusters in the country are\npending with the Government, if so, the details thereof, State-wise; and\n(d) the response of the Government thereon?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (IC) OF THE MINISTRY OF AYURVEDA,\nYOGA & NATUROPATHY, UNANI, SIDDHA AND\nHOMOEOPATHY(AYUSH)\n(SHRI SHRIPAD YESSO NAIK)\n(a) to (d):  A statement is laid on the Table of the House\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO 194 FOR 06TH MAY, 2016\n(a): So far, the Government has sanctioned 10 AYUSH Industry Clusters in the\ncountry. The details of funds allocated to each AYUSH Cluster is at Annexure.\n(b): Government is implementing a Central Sector Scheme for AYUSH Clusters\nin the 12th Plan period (2012-17) for setting up more such clusters in the country.\nUnder this Scheme, Government provides financial assistance upto 60% of the\nproject cost subject to a maximum of Rs.15 crores to the Special Purpose Vehicle\n(SPV) formed by at least 15 enterprises located in an existing cluster, that is, a group\nof AYUSH enterprises located in an area covering a radius of 100 Km . The projects\nare awarded based on the pro posal received not state -wise. However, preference is\ngiven to states where such SPVs do not exist.\n(c) & (d): No proposal from any of the State/UTs is pending with this Ministry.\n*****\n\nAnnexure\nS.\nNo\nName of the\nState Name of SPVs with Address\nTotal\nProject\nCost\nTotal Grant-\nin-aid\nSanctioned\nTotal Grant-\nin-aid\nReleased\n1 Karnataka\nAyurpark Health Care Limited, 168,\nChelakere Kalyan Nagar, Bangalore,\nKarnataka\n1736.00 1000.00\n991.82\n2 Kerala\nCARE Keralam, Kinfra Small Industries Park,\nNalukettu Road, Kinfra Park P.O., Thrissur,\nKerala\n1692.00 1000.00\n968.81\n3 Punjab\nHerbal Health Research Consortium Pvt. Ltd.\n277, East Mohan Nagar, Amritsar, Punjab 1679.00 1000.00\n985.00\n4 Rajasthan\nM/s Ayushraj Enterprises Pvt. Ltd;  35,\nSushilpura South, Ajmer Road Bridge, Shyam\nNagar 2nd Lane, Sodala, Jaipur, Rajasthan\n1620.00 970.00\n952.34\n5\nMaharashtra\nKonkan Ayur Pharma Pvt. Ltd.Gat No. 144,\nA/p.: Dhamani, Tal.: Sangameshwar, Dist. :\nRatnagiri, Maharashtra\n1479.00 888.00\n862.60\n6\nMaharashtra Ayurved Center Pvt. Ltd.,\nAradhana, 55, Amchi Colony, Pashan - NDA\nRoad, Bawdhan khurd, Pune, Maharashtra\n1582.00 949.00\n770.24\n7 Uttrakhand\nSanskar Ayush Medicare Pvt Ltd; Arya\nNagar, Jawalapur, Haridwar, Uttrakhand 1785.00 1000.00\n600.00\n8 Tamilnadu\nTraditional Ayurveda Cluster of Tamilnadu,\n48, Grand West Trunk Road, Kancheepuram\nDist., Sriperumbudur, Tamilnadu\n1659.00 965.00\n499.00\n9 Orissa\nRushikulya Ayurvedic Cluster pvt Ltd,\nAjodhya Nagar, 2nd lane, Berhampur,\nGanjam, Orissa\n999.00 599.00\n120.00\n10 Andhra\nPradesh\nLepakshi Ayush Park Private Limited, 5 -72\nPVC Colony, Bellary Road, Anantapur,\nAndhra Pradesh\n1801.00 1000.00\n200.00\nTOTAL 16032.00 9371.00 6949.81",
    "ls|LS16_S8_U_195": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 195\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 06TH MAY, 2016\nMISSION INDRADHANUSH\n*195. SHRI AJAY MISRA TENI:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to\nstate:\n(a) the salient features of the immunization programme, Mission Indradhanush;\n(b) whether the Government has received any proposals from States to include some\nmore vaccines under the mission, if so, the details the reof and the action taken\nthereon;\n(c) whether the Government has assessed the achievements made under the said\nprogramme, if so, the details and the outcome thereof; and\n(d) whether the Government proposes to run the programme on regular basis and\nalso integrate it with the vaccination programme for pregnant women for\nsafeguarding mother and child from diseases, if so, the details thereof and the steps\ntaken in this regard?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI JAGAT PRAKASH NADDA)\n(a) to (d):  A statement is laid on the Table of the House\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 195* FOR 06TH MAY, 2016\n(a): The Universal Immunization Programme, despite being operational since 1985 ,\nhas documented only 1% increase of immunization coverage per year in the last four\nyears (61% in CES 2009 to 65% in RSOC 2013 -14). Mission Indradhanush (MI)\nwas launched as a special drive to vaccinate all unvaccinate d and partially\nvaccinated children in high burden districts and rapidly improve immunization\ncoverage, with the objective to increase full immunization coverage to 90% and\nsustain the same by 2020.\n(b): No, the Government has not received any proposal fr om States to include some\nmore vaccines under this mission.\n(c): Yes, the Government has assessed the achievements made under Mission\nIndradhanush. Against an estimated target of 75,85,731 children, as on 30th April\n2016, 38,70,447 children were fully vac cinated in two Phases of Mission\nIndradhanush. In addition, 38,07,948 pregnant women were vaccinated against\ntetanus. This has resulted in at least 5% increase in full immunization coverage.\n(d): The Government plans to run Mission Indradhanush in the high priority districts\nto cover left out children and sustain the immunization coverage by 2020. The\nprogramme for vaccination of pregnant women with tetanus toxoid is already\nintegrated as evident from the details at (c) above.",
    "ls|LS16_S8_U_196": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 196 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 25.04.2016 \n \nSetting up of Central University in AP \n \n196. SHRI KESINENI NANI: \n \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government has any proposal to set up a new Central University in the State of \n Andhra Pradesh as envisaged in the Andhra Pradesh Re-organisation Act, 2014;  \n(b)  if so, the details thereof;  \n(c)  whether any survey has been conducted for selection of site for establishing the University \n and if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor;  \n(d)  whether the Government has made any financial allocation for the same during the current \n financial year and if so, the details thereof; and  \n(e)  the time by which it is likely to be implemented? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n \n(SMT. SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n \n (a) to (e): Yes, Madam. In pursuance of Andhra Pradesh Re -organisation Act, 2014, the Central \nGovernment has decided to es tablish a Central University in the State of Andhra Pradesh. A Site \nhas already been finalised for this purpose in Ananthapur District of the State.  Adequate funds \nwould be released by the Universit y Grants Commission in accordance with the requirement and \nphasing of establishment and construction activities.  No definite time period can  be given as \nestablishment of a new Central University depends on enactment of the relevant Bill. \n*** \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S8_U_197": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF CULTURE\n \nLOK\n \nSABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION  NO.\n197\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON \n25.04\n.2016\n \nVAISAKHA\n \n5, \n1938 (SAKA)\n \n \nPRESERVATION OF CULTURE \n \n \nWill the \nMinister  of \nCULTURE be pleased \nto state:\n \n \n                                                        \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE, CULTURE\n \nAND \nTOURISM (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \n \nAND  MINISTER OF STATE, CIVIL AVIATION\n \n \n(DR. MAHESH SHARMA)\n \n \n \n \n \n197\n.  \n \nSHRI RAVNEET SINGH: \n \nKUMARI SHOBHA KARANDLAJE\n:\n \n(a)\n \nwhether the Government has any special policy/scheme to preserve the tangible and \nintangible cultural heritage of the country;\n \n(b)\n \nif so, the main features of the policy and mechanism set up by the Government for \npreservation of tangible \nand intangible cultural heritage;\n \n \n(c)\n \nthe action taken by the Government in this regar\nd along with the measures taken\nfor \nvarious States including Punjab and the outcome thereof; and\n \n \n(d)\n \nthe funds allocated and utilised by the Government during the last\n \nfive years, State/UT\n-\nwise including Punjab in this regard?\n \n(a\n \nto\n \n(c)\n \nYes Madam. The Ministry of culture is responsible for preservation, development and \npromotion of both tangible and intangible heritage of India, which it implements through \nits schemes and Organisations located in various States of the country. \n \nWith r\negard to tangible heritage, the Ministry ensures preservation and  conservation of \ncentrally protected monuments of national importance through Archaeological Survey of \nIndia in terms of provisions contained in the Ancient Monuments & Archaeological Sites \nand Remains Act, 1958 and amendments thereunder.  Similarly, the Ministry promotes \nthe museum movement in the country through various Museums under its administrative \ncontrol and also through grant\n-\nin\n-\naids under Museum Grant Scheme. \n \nRegarding intangible c\nultural heritage, the Ministry extends financial support to \nindividuals, groups of individuals and cultural organizations engaged in performing visual \nand literary arts directly as well as through Organizations under its control. Also, a \nscheme named “Sche\nme for Safeguarding Intangible Cultural Heritage and Diverse \nCultural Traditions of India” was launched in 2013\n-\n14, which covers all recognised \ndomains of ICH such as oral traditions and expressions, including language as a vehicle \nof the intangible cultur\nal heritage, Performing Arts, Social Practices, rituals and festive \nevents, Knowledge and practices concerning nature and universe, traditional \ncraftsmanship etc. \n \n \n(d)\n \nThe Government does not allocate funds State and UT\n-\nwise. Funds are provided to the \nOrganizations under the Ministry of Culture located in various parts of the country in the \n form of grant - in - aid for fulfilling the mandate of the Ministry.  ",
    "ls|LS16_S8_U_200": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF AYURVEDA, YOGA & NATUROPATHY, UNANI, SIDDHA\nAND HOMOEOPATHY (AYUSH)\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 200\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 06TH MAY, 2016\nAYUSH PRODUCTS/MEDICINES\n*200. SHRI C. MAHENDRAN:\nSHRI P. NAGARAJAN:\nWill the Minister of AYURVEDA, YOGA AND NATUROPATHY, UNANI,\nSIDDHA AND HOMOEOPATHY (AYUSH) be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government has taken various steps to promote and popularise\nAYUSH system of medicines internationally, if so, the details thereof;\n(b) the details of countries that have shown interest in AYUSH systems of medicine\nand the success achieved in promoting and popularising AYUSH systems of\nmedicine internationally;\n(c) whether any quality regulations/ standards have been laid down f or use of heavy\nmetals in Ayurveda and other AYUSH products/medicines as those are detrimental\nto human health; and\n(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (IC) OF THE MINISTRY OF AYURVEDA,\nYOGA & NATUROPATHY, UNANI, SIDDHA AND\nHOMOEOPATHY(AYUSH)\n(SHRI SHRIPAD YESSO NAIK)\n(a) to (d):  A statement is laid on the Table of the House\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 200 FOR 06TH MAY, 2016\n(a) & (b):  In line with its mandate for global promotion and propagation of the\nAYUSH systems of medicine, the Ministry of AYUSH has been organizing/\nparticipating in International exhibitions/ conferences/ workshops/ seminars/ road\nshows/ trade fairs, etc. to have wider reach for international propagation of AYUSH.\nIncentives are also being provided (a) to AYUSH drug manufacturers, entrepreneurs,\nAYUSH institutions, etc. for participating in International exhibitions/ conferences/\nworkshops/ seminars/ road shows/ trade fairs, etc. for generating awareness amongst\nthe participating public about the Indian Medicine and; (b) to AYUSH drug\nmanufacturers for registration of AYUSH products with regulatory agencies of\ndifferent countries to enhance export of the prod ucts. To meet the worldwide\nincreasing demand of Yoga experts, the Ministry of AYUSH has launched a scheme\nfor Voluntary Certification of Yoga Professionals on 22nd June, 2015 in\ncollaboration with Quality Council of India (QCI).  The Scheme aims at promot ing\nauthentic Yoga as a preventive and health promotive drugless therapy and involves\ncertifying the competence level of the professionals to help their deployment within\nand outside the country.\nAs a result of concerted efforts Ministry of AYUSH has ach ieved great\nsuccess by marking presence of AYUSH systems of medicine across the globe, as is\nevident from the following:\n(i)   Memorandums of Understanding (MoUs) for Country to Country cooperation in\nfield of Traditional Medicine have been signed with Nep al, Bangladesh, Hungary,\nTrinidad & Tobago, Malaysia, China, Mauritius, Mongolia and Turkmenistan.\n(ii)  MoUs for undertaking collaborative research have been signed with Royal\nLondon Hospital for Integrated Medicine, UK and National Centre for Natural\nProduct Research (NCNPR) University of Mississippi, USA and College of\nHomeopaths of Ontario, Canada; and Letters of Intent (LoIs) with Mexico and\nUniversity of Strasbourg, France.\n(iii)  The Ministry has also set up Academic Chairs in Hungary, Trinidad & T obago\n(Ayurveda Chair) and South Africa (Unani Chair).  Besides, MoUs have also been\n\nsigned with Universities in Russia, Indonesia, Slovenia, Thailand and Armenia for\nsetting up of AYUSH Academic Chairs.\n(iv) AYUSH Information Cells for dissemination of a uthentic information about\nAYUSH Systems of Medicine have been set up at Malaysia, Trinidad & Tobago,\nMexico, Cuba, Hungary, Russia, Indonesia (2 Cells), Mauritius, Slovenia, Sweden,\nChina, Dubai, Croatia, Kyrgyzstan, Argentina and Israel.\n(v) Recently, t he Union Cabinet has approved the signing of Agreement with the\nWorld Health Organisation (WHO) for long term collaboration under which, the\nWHO would help in improving international acceptability and promotion of AYUSH\nsystems.\n(vi)  An Indo-US workshop on Traditional Systems with special focus on cancer was\norganized in New Delhi on 3-4 March, 2016. A US team comprising of experts from\nNational Cancer Institute (NCI) took part in the two-day exhaustive deliberations.\n(c) & (d) : Drugs & Cosmetics Act, 1 940 and Rules thereunder have regulatory\nprovisions for Ayurvedic, Siddha and Unani (ASU) medicines, under which it is\nmandatory for the manufacturers to comply with the quality standards of ingredients\nof minerals and metallic origin as prescribed in the pharmacopoeia  and with Good\nManufacturing Practices (GMP) for manufacturing of herbo -mineral-metallic\ncompounds of Ayurveda, Siddha and Unani medicines as contained in part ‘D’  of\nSchedule ‘T’ of the Drugs & Cosmetics Rules, 1945.\nAlso, permissible lim its of heavy metals in ASU medicines manufactured\nfrom plant materials are prescribed in the pharmacopoeia.\nIt is also pertinent to state that heavy metals are used in the manufacturing of\nASU medicines after subjecting them to certain sophisticated phar maceutical\nprocesses called the Shodhana, Marana, Bhavana, Amritikarana and trituration\nwith juices or decoctions of medicinal plants  to render them therapeutically safe and\neffective with judicious consumption under medical supervision.  Labelling\nprovisions for such ASU medicines in the Drugs & Cosmetics Rules, 1945 seek\nmention of the ‘Caution: to be taken under medical supervision’ on the container of\nmedicine.",
    "ls|LS16_S8_U_203": " \n \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION No. 203  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 9TH MAY, 2016 \nOpen and Distance Learning Institutions \n*203. SHRI GODSE HEMANT TUKARAM:  \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:  \n(a) whether the Government is promoting Open and Distance Education in the country, if so, the \ndetails thereof;  \n(b) the major streams/disciplines of distance education; \n(c) the names of conventional/Deemed Univer sities having Open and Distance Learning \nInstitutions in the country, State/ UT-wise;  \n(d) whether the Programmes/Courses run by these Un iversities have the consent/ approval of the \nDistance Education Council and if so, the details thereof; and \n(e) if not, the action taken by the Government against the defaulting universities? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(SMT. SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n \n(a) to (e): A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n3rd POSITION \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PA RTS (A), (B), (C), (D) & (E) OF THE \nLOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 203 FOR 9 TH MAY, 2016 ASKED BY SHRI \nGODSE HEMANT TUKARAM REGARDING OPEN AND DISTANCE LEARNING \nINSTITUTIONS \n(a): Yes, Madam. At present there is one Central University i.e. Indira Gandhi National Open \nUniversity (IGNOU) and fourteen (14) public f unded State Open Universities (SOUs) apart from \nthe Distance Education Institutions (DEIs) of the Conventional Universities and Deemed \nUniversities offering courses/programmes in the field of Open & Distance Learning (ODL) in the \ncountry. The details are given in Annexure-I. The Central Government provides some Plan \nFunds towards development of course material and quality assurance, student support services at \nthe Head Quarter and Regional Centres, staff trai ning and development, technical support, library \nfacilities and its augmentation, creation of e-content etc. to State Open Universities (SOUs) \nthrough the University Grants Commission (UGC) which is the regulatory authority for \nmonitoring and maintenance of standards in the Open and Distance Education in the country.  \n(b): The major streams/disciplines of ODL  programmes/courses offered by various \nUniversities/Deemed Universities as approved by the UGC in the field of ODL education are \nHumanities, Social Sciences and General Sciences etc.   \n(c): As per the information furnished by UGC, the State-wise names of the Conventional/Deemed \nUniversities which are approved by the UGC to offer specific programmes through Open and \nDistance Learning mode during the academic year 2015-16 is given at Annexure-I. Further, UGC \nwhile approving the proposals of Conventional/Deemed Universities for running ODL \nprogrammes/courses maintains its territorial jurisdiction policy. \n(d): Yes, Madam. The universities as refere d above can offer only those programmes/courses \nthrough ODL mode which are specifically mentioned in the approval letter addressed to the \nparticular university by UGC from time to time. \n(e): With a view to maintain standards and quality in running ODL programmes/courses by \nvarious Conventional/Deemed Universities, UGC as a regulator examines all complaints received \nby it, if any, and based on the gravity and seri ousness of the complaint(s), issues warning(s), \nshow-cause notice(s) and even withdrawal of the ODL programmes/courses of such universities \nfor not adhering to the UGC guidelines.  The UGC also issues public notices etc. for the use of \ngeneral public to make them aware about the issues of territorial jurisdiction and validity of \nrecognition of the ODL programmes/courses. \n\n******* \n \nANNEXURE-I \nANNEXURE IN REPLY TO PART ‘A’ AND ‘C’ OF THE LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. 203 FOR ANSWER ON 09.05.2016 ASKED BY SHRI GODSE HEMANT \nTUKARAM REGARDING OPEN AND DISTANCE LEARNING INSTITUTIONS \nLIST OF UNIVERSITIES / INSTITUTIONS WHICH WERE APPROVED BY UGC TO OFFER \nPROGRAMMES THROUGH OPEN AND DISTANCE LEARNING MODE DURING THE \nACADEMIC YEAR 2015-16  As on 04.05.2016 \nS.No State Name of University/ Institution Type of \nUniversity/Institute \n1 Andhra Pradesh Andhra University State University \n2 Andhra Pradesh Dr. Bhim Rao Ambedkar Open University State University \n3 Andhra Pradesh Dravidian University State University \n4 Andhra Pradesh Gandhi Institute of Technology and \nManagement (GITAM) \nDeemed University \n5 Andhra Pradesh Sri Venkateswara University State University \n6 Andhra Pradesh University of Hyderabad Central University \n7 Andhra Pradesh Rashtriya Sanskrit Vidyapeetha Tirupati Deemed to be University \n8 Arunachal Pradesh Rajiv Gandhi University Central University \n9 Assam Assam Don Bosco University Private University \n10 Assam Tezpur University Central University \n11 Assam Assam Down Town University Private University \n12 Assam Dibrugarh University State University \n13 Assam Krishana Kanta Handiqui State Open University State University \n14 Assam Gauhati University Guwahat State University \n15 Bihar Babasaheb Bhimrao Ambedkar Bihar \nUniversity \nState University \n16 Bihar Jai Prakash Vishwavidyalaya State University \n17 Bihar Magadh University State University \n18 Bihar Nalanda Open University State University \n19 Bihar Patna University State University \n20 Bihar  Lalit Narayan Mithila University State University \n21 Chhattisgarh Dr. C.V. Raman University Private University \n22 Chhattisgarh Pt.Sundarlal Sharma Open University  State University \n23 Delhi All India Management Association Private Institute \n24 Delhi Indira Gandhi National Open University \n(IGNOU) \nCentral University \n25 Delhi Jamia Mallia Islamia University Central University \n26 Delhi Rashtriya Sanskrit Sansthana Deemed University \n27 Delhi TERI School of Advanced Studies Deemed University \n28 Delhi University of Delhi Central 