    "ls|LS16_S8_U_220": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY  \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 220 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON  25.04.2016  \n \nCut in Scholarships \n  \n220.  SHRI HARISH MEENA:    \n \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n  \n(a) whether it is a fact that Government has made a drastic cut in amount given to States \nfor pre-matric, post-matric and merit -cum-means scholarships to Students belonging to \nSCs/STs/OBCs/Minorities; \n(b) if so,  the details thereof along with the reasons therefor; and  \n(c) the details of grants sought by State Governments and sanctioned grants by c entre to \nState in past two years ?  \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(SMT. SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n(a) to (c) : The Ministries of Social Justice and Empowerment,  Tribal Affairs and Mino rity \nAffairs are administering Pre- matric, Post -matric and Merit -cum-Means scholarship \nschemes for students belonging to SCs/STs/OBCs/Minorities.  The Department of School \nEducation and Literacy is administering two scholarship schemes namely National Means -\ncum-Merit Scholarship Scheme and National Scheme of Incentive to Girls for Secondary \nEducation for students belonging to economically weaker sections of the society. The \nMinistry/ Department-wise details are as follows:  \nDepartment of Social Justice & Empowerment :  \nThe Central Assistance under the Centrally Sponsored Post-Matric Scholarship Scheme for \nSC students during last 2 years has  been released as per the no tional allocation made to \nthe States each year . Central Assistance released under this scheme wa s Rs. 196337 .63 \nlakhs during 2014-15 and Rs. 221388.00 lakhs during 2015-16.  \nThe Central Assistance of Pre- matric Scholarship for SC students studying in classes IX & \nX during the last two year s has been released after taking into consideration the unspent \nfunds available with the States /UTs. Central Assistance has also been released on DBT \nmode during the last 2 years based on the details furnished by the States/UTs. Central  \n\n \nAssistance released under this scheme wa s Rs. 51403.34 lakhs during 2014-15  and Rs. \n52470.31 lakhs during 2015-16.  \nMinistry of Tribal Affairs \nFor Post-Matric Scholarships, Rs. 72100.82 lakhs and Rs. 85714.54 lakhs had been \nreleased against the demand by States amounting  to Rs. 177482.14 lakhs and Rs. \n194202.43 lakhs in 2014-15 and 2015-16 respectively.  \nFor Pre -Matric Scholarships, Rs. 20070.87 lakhs and Rs. 22868.95 lakhs had been \nreleased against the demand by States amounting to Rs. 36447.61 lakhs and Rs.45288.61 \nlakhs in 2014-15 and 2015-16 respectively.  \nMinistry of Minority Affairs \nGovernment has not made any cut in amount for scholarship under Pre- Matric, Post-Matric \nand Merit -Cum-Means scholarship schemes o f this Ministry. Th e funds for s cholarship \nunder Pre-Matric for the year 2014- 15 was given to all State Governments for release of \nscholarship fund amount to the eligible students.  A total fund of Rs.1129.27 Crore was \nreleased to State Government s during 2014 -15 and the fun d for Pre -Matric Scholarship \nScheme is being released directly to the students bank accounts through DBT  w. e. f. \n2015-16, whereas Post -Matric and Merit -Cum-Means scholarship are being distributed \nthrough DBT w.e.f  2014-15. \nDepartment of School Education and Literacy   \nUnder Centrally Sponsored National Means -cum-Merit Scholarship Scheme, Rs. 65.20 \ncrore and Rs. 127.13  crore had been sanctioned for scholarships against the requirement \nof funds  amounting to Rs. 159.82  crore and Rs. 109.38 crore in 2014- 15 and 2015- 16 \nrespectively. The funds released in 2015- 16 also included release of funds for pending \nscholarships received in 2014-15. The scholarships are sanctioned as per the availability of \nthe budget. The scholarship amount is transferred directly into the students bank accounts \nand scholarship funds are not released to the State Governments. \nUnder Centrally Sponsored National Scheme of Incentive to Girls for Secondary Education, \nRs. 94.23 crore and Rs. 153.54 crore h ad been sanctioned as  incentive amount for girl \nstudents against the requirement of funds amounting to Rs. 169.19 crore and Rs. 288.29 \ncrore in 2014- 15 and 2015- 16 respectively . The scholarships are sanctioned as per  the \navailability of the budget. The incentive amount is transferred directly into the students bank \naccounts and funds are not released to the State Governments. \n****** \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S8_U_221": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.†221 \nANSWERED ON 25.04.2016 \n \nSPECIAL TOURIST SECURITY FORCE \n \n†221. SHRI KUNWAR PUSHPENDRA SINGH CHANDEL:  \n \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  whether the Governm ent is adopting any special measures for \nthe safety of Historical sites and idols in temples at tourist \nplaces;  \n(b)  if so, the details thereof;  \n(c)  whether the Government proposes to use technical \napplications and constitute a special tourist place sec urity \nforce for the protection of such tourist places;  \n(d)  if so, the details thereof; and  \n(e)  the action taken by the Government in this regard? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR TOURISM (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \n(DR. MAHESH SHARMA) \n \n(a) to (e): The primary r esponsibility, including the safety of \nHistorical sites and idols, is in the domain of the respective \nState/UT Government.  However, the Government of India provides \nCentral Financial Assistance to the States and UTs for the purpose.  \nFurther, a committee of Ministry of Culture has recommended a \nSpecial Force along with the help of tools and electronic gadgets for \nprotection of Monuments and Art Installations as part of a \ncomprehensive security policy. \n \n******** ",
    "ls|LS16_S8_U_222": "Government of India \nMinistry of Consumer Affairs, Food and Public Distribution \nDepartment of Consumer Affairs \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *222 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 10.05.2016 \n \nRISE IN PRICES OF ESSENTIAL COMMODITIES \n \n*222.  SHRI PRALHAD JOSHI:  \nSHRI B.S. YEDIYURAPPA:  \n \nWill the Minister of CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIC \nDISTRIBUTION miHkksDrk ekeys] [kk| vkSj lkoZtfud forj.k ea=h be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether prices of essential commodities including those monitored by Price Monitoring \nCell continued to rise over a period of time despite various steps taken by the \nGovernment, if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor;  \n(b)  whether the Government has received all necessary information/logistics on price trends \nduring the last three years, if so, the details thereof and the action taken thereon;  \n(c)  the steps taken by the Union Government to check hoarding and ensure compliance with \nvarious rules related to supply of essential commodities and price control by the States, \nif so, the details and the outcome thereof; and  \n(d) whether any assessment has b een made about impact of water shortage on prices and if \nso, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nmiHkksDrk ekeys] [kk| vkSj lkoZtfud forj.k ea=ky; ea=h  \n¼Jh jke foykl ikloku½ \n \nTHE MINISTER OF  \nCONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n(SHRI RAM VILAS PASWAN) \n \n(a) to (d) :    A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.  \n \n***** \n  \n\n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (d) OF LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO.*  222 FOR 10.05.2016 REGARDING RISE IN PRICES OF ESSENTIAL \nCOMMODITIES. \n------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ \n \n(a) :  Prices of most of the essential food items monitored by Price Monitoring Cell (PMC) has \nremained relatively stable or declined over the year. The food items which showed an increase \nin prices include pulses (especially tur, urad and chana), potato and suga r. Increase in prices of \nfood items is mainly on account of shortfall in production owing to adverse weather conditions, \nincreased transportation costs, supply chain constraints like lack of storage facilities and \nartificial shortage created by hoarding and black marketing. The details are at Annnexure I. \n \n(b)  :  Yes sir. The requisite information is given at Annexure II & III.  \n \n(c) : States have been advised to take effective action against hoarders of essential \ncommodities under the Essential Commoditie s Act, 1955 and the Prevention of Black -\nMarketing and Maintenance of Supplies of Essential Commodities Act 1980. States/UTs have \nbeen conducting raids and seized pulses are being disposed, as per the provisions under the EC \nAct, 1955. The Action Taken Report is given at Annexure IV. \n \n(d) : Water shortage adversely affects the sown area and productivity of crops. The resultant \nshortfall in production in turn affects the quantum of market arrivals and thereby prices. \nHowever, no official assessment of direct impact of water shortage on prices has been made. \n \n***** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \nANNEXURE-I \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED IN REPLY TO PART (a) OF LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO.* 222 FOR 10.05.2016 REGARDING RISE IN PRICES OF ESSENTIAL \nCOMMODITIES. \n------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- \nALL INDIA AVERAGE RETAIL PRICES (Rs./Kg) \n \nCommodities Prices as on \n(5/5/2016)* \nPrices as on \n(5/5/2015) \nPercent \nchange \nRice 26.92 27.29 -1.36 \nWheat 23.3 22.06 5.62 \nAtta (Wheat) 24.49 24.34 0.62 \nGram Dal 72.33 53.5 35.2 \nTur/Arhar Dal 141.38 87.92 60.81 \nUrad Dal 149.39 86.37 72.97 \nMoong Dal 101.34 100.97 0.37 \nMasoor Dal 83.07 76.33 8.83 \nSugar 38.93 31.85 22.23 \nMilk @ 40.25 38.93 3.39 \nGroundnut Oil (Packed) 128.28 120.9 6.1 \nMustard Oil (Packed) 108.95 99.6 9.39 \nVanaspati (Packed) 73.22 75.55 -3.08 \nSoya Oil (Packed) 82.09 85.32 -3.79 \nSunflower Oil (Packed) 95.14 94.48 0.7 \nPalm Oil (Packed) 69.32 67.58 2.57 \nGur  40.93 40.29 1.59 \nTea Loose 197.67 205.05 -3.6 \nSalt Pack (Iodised) 14.8 14.65 1.02 \nPotato 18.52 13.48 37.39 \nOnion 16.33 22.02 -25.84 \nTomato 22.8 25.08 -9.09 \n \nNR -> Not Reported      * -->    (Iodised) @ -->    (Rs./Ltr.)      *latest available \nSource:-  State civil supplies Department \n***** \n  \n\n \nANNEXURE-II \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED IN REPLY TO PART (b) OF LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. \n*222 FOR 10.5.2016 REGARDING RISE IN PRICES OF ESSENTIAL COMMODITIES. \n------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ \nRETAIL AND WHOLESALE PRICES OF ESSENTIAL COMMODITIES FOR LAST THREE \nYEARS. \n \nSl.No. Commodities ALL INDIA AVERAGE RETAIL PRICES \n(Rs./Kg)  \nALL INDIA AVERAGE WHOLESALE \nPRICES (Rs./Qtl)  \n2016-17 2015-16 2014-15 2016-17 2015-16 2014-15 \nas on (5/5/2016)* as on \n(5/5/2015) \nas on \n(5/5/2014) \nas on \n(5/5/2016) \nas on \n(5/5/2015) \nas on \n(5/5/2014) \n1 Rice 26.92 27.29 27.08 2410.66 2442.5 2447.56 \n2 Wheat 23.3 22.06 20.81 2066.96 1988.78 1858.4 \n3 Atta (Wheat) 24.49 24.34 22.94 2164.29 2154.92 2017.7 \n4 Gram Dal 72.33 53.5 47.96 6721.13 4909.63 4343.62 \n5 Tur/Arhar Dal 141.38 87.92 69.92 13278.55 8345.58 6552.45 \n6 Urad Dal 149.39 86.37 66.78 14006.1 8142.28 6176.17 \n7 Moong Dal 101.34 100.97 87.76 9355.12 9482.69 8246.36 \n8 Masoor Dal 83.07 76.33 63.04 7643.1 7110.42 5818.14 \n9 Sugar 38.93 31.85 36.12 3621.02 2894.83 3374.67 \n10 Milk @ 40.25 38.93 36.51 3748.83 3704.92 3471.13 \n11 Groundnut Oil \n(Packed) \n128.28 120.9 121.61 12090.62 11379.4 11355.58 \n12 Mustard Oil \n(Packed) \n108.95 99.6 97.38 9981.67 9242.74 8907.23 \n13 Vanaspati \n(Packed) \n73.22 75.55 76.58 6792.69 6969.32 7163.71 \n14 Soya Oil \n(Packed) \n82.09 85.32 85.46 7609.45 7774.88 7900.21 \n15 Sunflower Oil \n(Packed) \n95.14 94.48 95.21 9017.24 8873.96 8951.42 \n16 Palm Oil \n(Packed) \n69.32 67.58 71.56 6516.3 6252.21 6760.38 \n17 Gur 40.93 40.29 39.12 3658.75 3496.9 3463.32 \n18 Tea Loose 197.67 205.05 205.11 18024.03 18322.7 17650 \n19 Salt Pack \n(Iodised) \n14.8 14.65 14.24 1249.75 1266.13 1229.07 \n20 Potato 18.52 13.48 19.64 1467.47 1021.05 1532.77 \n21 Onion 16.33 22.02 17.96 1221.42 1711.63 1369.96 \n22 Tomato 22.8 25.08 18.58 1796.18 1918.1 1380.84 \n   * -->    (Iodised) @ -->    (Rs./Ltr.)       * latest available \nSource:-  State civil supplies Department \n***** \n \n \n \n\nANNEXURE- III \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED IN REPLY TO PART (b) OF LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. *222 FOR \n10.5.2016 REGARDING RISE IN PRICES OF ESSENTIAL COMMODITIES. \n---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- \nSteps taken by the Government to improve the availability and to contain prices of essential food items: \n \nGeneral Measure: \n \n1. National Consultation Meeting of the minister of states/UTs in -charge of Consumer Affairs and Food held on \n7thJuly, 2015 at New Delhi resolved to take steps to keep prices of essential commodities, especially Pulse s \nand onion under control. \n2. Advisory were issued to State Governments to take strict action against hoarding & black marketing and \neffectively enforce the Essential Commodities Act, 1955 & the Prevention of Black -marketing and \nMaintenance of Supplies of Essential Commodities Act, 1980.  \n3. Regular review meeting on price and availability situation is being held at the highest level includ ing at the \nlevel of Finance Minister, Committee of Secretaries, Inter Ministerial Committee, Price Stabilizatio n Fund \nManagement Committee and other Departmental level review meetings. \n4. Higher MSP has been announced so as to incentivize production and thereby enhance availability of fo od \nitems which may help moderate prices. \n5. A Plan Scheme titled Price Stabilization Fund (PSF) is being implemented to regulate price volatility of \nagricultural commodities. \n \nSpecific Measures: \n \nOnion \n6. Export of onion were restricted through imposition of Minimum Export Price (MEP) when prices were ru ling \nhigh and import is allowed at zero duty. \n7. Retail sale of onion was undertaken from the stock held by SFAC and NAFED. \n8. Imported 2000 MT of onion from Egypt and China through MMTC. As no demands from state were received,  \nthe onion were disposed off through tender in the open market. \n9. The stock limits in respect of onion have been extend ed by one more year i.e. up to 2nd July 2016 under the \nEssential Commodities Act. \n10. Procurement of 15000 MT of onion by SFAC and NAFED for market intervention during lean period has been \napproved. \nPulses  \n11. Export of all pulses is banned except kabuli channa and up to 10,000 MTs in organic pulses and lentils.  \n12. Import of pulses are allowed at zero import duty.  \n13. Stock limit on pulses extended till 30.9.2016.   \n14. Government imported 5000 MT of Tur from Malawi/Mozambique and allocated it to States for retail sale  to \nconsumers to improve availability and to moderate prices. \n15. MSP (including bonus) raised for kharif pulses for Tur and Urad and Moong. MSP also raised for Rabi pulses \nfor Gram and Masoor. \n16. Government has approved creation of buffer stock of 1.5 lakh MT of pulses for effective market intervention. \n17. Government has decided to immediately release 10,000 MT of pulses from the buffer stock (consisting of \n8,000 MT of Tur and 2,000 MT of Urad) to States/UTs at subsidized rates for retailing by them at not  more \nthan Rs 120/- per kg to improve availability and stabilise prices. \n18. Regulatory measures by  Securities & Exchange Board of India (SEBI) on Chana contracts including inc rease \nin the margin requirement to discourage speculation and to moderate the price volatility in  forward market and \nclose monitoring by SEBI \n19. Strict vigilance by Directorate of Revenue Intelligence to prevent importers from mis -using the facilities of \nCustoms Bonded Warehouse facility \n20. Setting up of a Group of Officers for regular monitoring and exchange of information on hoarding, \ncartelization etc. \n \nEdible Oils \n21. Export of edible oils in bulk is prohibited except coconut oil and other edible oils in branded cons umer packs \nof up to 5 kgs is permitted with a minimum export price of USD 900 per MT. \n22. MSP increased for various rabi and kharif oil seeds \n***** \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \nANNEXURE-IV \n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED IN REPLY TO PART (c) OF LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. *222 FOR \n10.5.2016 REGARDING RISE IN PRICES OF ESSENTIAL COMMODITIES. \n---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- \n \nACTION TAKEN UNDER THE ESSENTIAL COMMODITIES ACT, 1955 DURING 2016 \n(Relating to offences under EC Act- for other than violation of stock control orders/ for violation of \nstock control orders) \n       Updated as on 03.05.2016  \n  Note: Other States have not submitted report so far., NR  : Not Reported,               \n* :  Raids under Pulses only \n \n*****\nSl. \nNo. STATES/UTs \nNo. of \nRaids \nConducted \nNo. of Persons Value of \ngoods \nConfiscated \n(Rs. In \nLakhs) \nDetentions \nOrdered \nReported \nupto Arrested Prosecuted Convicted \n1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 9 \n1. Arunachal \nPradesh Nil Nil Nil Nil Nil  January \n2. Delhi 1 - - - -  January \n3. Goa 8 Nil Nil Nil Nil  March \n4. Gujarat 1641 0 1 - 21.29 4 February \n5. Haryana 17 - - - -  * \n6. Himachal \nPradesh 3912 - - - 5.03  February \n7. Kerala 10 4 1 0 0.10  January \n8. Maharashtra 75 93 45 0 26.95  February \n9. Manipur Nil Nil Nil Nil Nil  January \n10. Mizoram 3 - - - -  January